WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:08.480
You know, when we usually talk about getting organized or cleaning up a mess, we really tend to think about hiding things.

00:08.660 --> 00:10.080
Right, yeah, just sweeping it away.

00:10.240 --> 00:16.940
Exactly. Like, imagine you have company coming over in 10 minutes, and your living room is just an absolute disaster. What do you do?

00:17.060 --> 00:18.200
Panic, mostly.

00:18.380 --> 00:27.140
Right, you panic, and then you take that massive pile of mail, the receipts, the scattered magazines, and you just sort of shove them all under a clean conceptual rug,

00:27.140 --> 00:33.240
or you toss them into a junk drawer, slam it shut, and just lean against it smiling when they walk in.

00:33.300 --> 00:34.500
Yeah, out of sight, out of mind.

00:34.660 --> 00:35.000
Exactly.

00:35.180 --> 00:45.600
And on the surface, everything looks pristine, the room is, you know, clean, but underneath that rug, inside that drawer, the chaos is still exactly the same.

00:45.680 --> 00:48.160
It's completely unresolved, it's merely been concealed.

00:48.480 --> 00:52.860
Right. If you open that drawer tomorrow, you still have to deal with the exact same mess.

00:52.860 --> 00:58.440
And for decades, I mean, that is pretty much how we've been taught to think about the concept of abstraction.

00:59.000 --> 01:00.760
It is, across almost every field.

01:01.120 --> 01:09.380
Yeah, whether we're talking about computer programming or mathematics, or even just how we organize our daily lives, we treat abstraction like that living room rug.

01:09.620 --> 01:14.660
We assume it means hiding the messy, complicated details of the world so we just don't have to look at them.

01:14.860 --> 01:18.920
Right, we put a clean label on a box and try to forget about the tangled wires inside.

01:18.920 --> 01:24.120
But today, we are going to completely flip that idea on its head.

01:24.320 --> 01:25.760
Welcome to the Deep Dive.

01:25.880 --> 01:28.400
And we really have quite the journey ahead of us today.

01:28.560 --> 01:29.220
We really do.

01:29.740 --> 01:33.540
Today, we're unpacking the work of a researcher and author named Flixion.

01:33.940 --> 01:37.920
And we're looking at thousands of pages of recent 2025 monographs.

01:38.760 --> 01:39.920
Dense, groundbreaking texts.

01:40.680 --> 01:41.320
Very dense.

01:41.500 --> 01:45.540
Texts with incredibly intimidating titles like Abstraction as Reduction.

01:45.540 --> 01:54.140
And we're also pulling from a few of their supplemental essays, things with titles like Compressed Causality and From Synchrony to Structure.

01:54.340 --> 01:54.500
Right.

01:54.600 --> 02:01.280
And the core of all this material is a unified theory that argues our entire understanding of abstraction is completely backwards.

02:01.720 --> 02:02.180
It's wild.

02:02.520 --> 02:08.440
Flixion's central thesis is, um, it's as radical as it is mathematically rigorous.

02:09.120 --> 02:12.900
The argument is that abstraction is absolutely not the act of hiding details.

02:13.440 --> 02:13.980
Exactly.

02:13.980 --> 02:17.540
Instead, abstraction is the execution of detail.

02:17.660 --> 02:18.680
The execution of it.

02:18.820 --> 02:20.140
Which is such a weird phrase.

02:20.360 --> 02:21.880
To abstract is to compute.

02:22.540 --> 02:26.160
It is the act of resolution of complexity, not just, you know, concealing it.

02:26.220 --> 02:26.400
Right.

02:26.480 --> 02:28.300
And that sounds like a neat philosophical trick.

02:28.520 --> 02:32.420
But the sheer scale of what Flixion is proposing is just staggering.

02:32.700 --> 02:33.780
It really blew my mind.

02:34.160 --> 02:41.140
The claim is that this single mechanism, this mathematical rule of reducing complexity by executing it, governs literally everything.

02:41.140 --> 02:47.400
Everything from how your smartphone's processor crunches data to how your brain constructs your literal sense of identity.

02:47.400 --> 02:47.840
Right.

02:47.940 --> 02:52.400
All the way down to the fundamental thermodynamic forces that hold the physical universe together.

02:52.640 --> 02:54.900
It's framed as a unified theory of reality.

02:55.260 --> 02:59.500
Built on the idea that the universe is quite literally calculating itself into stability.

02:59.500 --> 03:02.280
It is an incredibly ambitious framework.

03:02.520 --> 03:05.940
And our goal today is to carefully connect those dots for you.

03:06.340 --> 03:17.840
We want to build a bridge from formal logic into the physical metal of computer hardware up into the human mind and finally plunge into quantum level physics.

03:17.840 --> 03:20.540
But we're going to do it by focusing on the why and the how.

03:20.680 --> 03:23.980
We aren't going to just throw, you know, complex equations at you.

03:24.100 --> 03:24.260
Yeah.

03:24.380 --> 03:25.040
Nobody wants that.

03:25.420 --> 03:27.400
We want to look at the mechanical structure of this argument.

03:27.620 --> 03:28.980
So let's start at the foundation.

03:29.240 --> 03:33.600
Because to understand this massive shift in perspective, we have to look at computer science.

03:33.900 --> 03:36.460
Specifically, a concept called the lambda calculus.

03:36.840 --> 03:37.100
Right.

03:37.220 --> 03:41.280
Now, if you're listening to this and you're not a programmer, please do not tune out.

03:41.280 --> 03:48.060
Stick with us, because the way Fliction frames this is going to change how you look at every single black box in your life.

03:48.180 --> 03:48.800
It really will.

03:49.360 --> 03:55.700
The foundational premise here is that abstraction is structurally identical to a concept called reduction in computer science.

03:55.860 --> 03:58.320
But what does that actually mean when we talk about lambda calculus?

03:58.620 --> 03:59.680
Like, give me the basics.

04:00.120 --> 04:04.340
Well, to understand that, we need to strip away almost everything you think you know about computers.

04:05.340 --> 04:11.140
The untyped lambda calculus is the purest distillation of computation that we have.

04:11.280 --> 04:12.180
Okay. Pure how?

04:12.400 --> 04:17.680
It was invented by a brilliant mathematician named Alonzo Church back in the 1930s.

04:17.920 --> 04:19.940
And when I say pure, I mean austere.

04:20.040 --> 04:20.760
There are no loops.

04:20.940 --> 04:22.220
There is no memory storage.

04:22.320 --> 04:22.660
Oh, okay.

04:22.920 --> 04:24.800
There aren't even basic data types.

04:24.800 --> 04:28.800
There are no numbers, no strings of text, no built-in logic operations.

04:29.680 --> 04:30.280
Wait, hold on.

04:30.400 --> 04:32.480
A mathematical system with no numbers?

04:32.660 --> 04:34.160
How do you even do math?

04:34.300 --> 04:34.600
I know.

04:34.680 --> 04:35.420
It sounds impossible.

04:35.700 --> 04:37.260
But you only have three things.

04:37.480 --> 04:39.560
You have variables, which are just placeholders.

04:39.700 --> 04:40.300
Okay, bury this.

04:40.300 --> 04:44.920
You have function creations, which are called lambda abstractions, essentially creating a rule.

04:45.100 --> 04:48.960
And you have function applications, which is feeding an input into a rule.

04:49.080 --> 04:49.580
And that's it.

04:49.640 --> 04:52.160
That is the entire universe of the lambda calculus.

04:52.420 --> 04:54.460
It is just a raw grammar of logic.

04:54.660 --> 04:59.740
Okay, so if I just have rules and inputs, how does anything actually get done?

05:00.100 --> 05:01.860
Like, how does a calculation move forward?

05:01.860 --> 05:07.700
Computation in the system happens through a single repetitive process called beta reduction.

05:07.860 --> 05:08.580
Beta reduction.

05:08.720 --> 05:09.860
Okay, picture that for me.

05:10.080 --> 05:15.260
Imagine you have a massive, complex mathematical expression written out on a blackboard.

05:15.480 --> 05:18.820
It's full of nested parentheses, just a huge wall of logic.

05:18.940 --> 05:20.020
Sounds like a nightmare, but okay.

05:20.020 --> 05:31.100
Beta reduction is the process of scanning that massive equation, identifying the innermost reducible expression, which computer scientists call a redex, and resolving it.

05:31.140 --> 05:32.260
The innermost expression.

05:32.400 --> 05:36.020
So you're drilling all the way down to the deepest, most nested part of the equation.

05:36.140 --> 05:36.780
Yes, exactly.

05:36.780 --> 05:44.020
You find the deepest function, you feed it its argument, you substitute that argument into the body of the function, and you evaluate it.

05:44.320 --> 05:46.100
You resolve that one tiny piece.

05:46.500 --> 05:46.880
Oh, I see.

05:47.200 --> 05:53.680
Then you step back, look at the slightly smaller equation, find the new innermost redex, and resolve that.

05:53.980 --> 05:57.780
You are constantly satisfying the deepest dependency first.

05:57.860 --> 06:00.860
And you just repeat this process over and over and over.

06:01.020 --> 06:01.680
Over and over.

06:01.840 --> 06:04.840
You keep chewing on the math until there's nothing left to chew on.

06:05.220 --> 06:05.740
Precisely.

06:05.740 --> 06:11.180
You keep applying beta reduction until you reach a state where no further reductions are possible.

06:11.420 --> 06:12.460
And what is that state called?

06:12.780 --> 06:16.900
That state has a very specific, very important name in mathematics.

06:17.500 --> 06:19.340
It is called normal form.

06:19.520 --> 06:21.620
Normal form, meaning it's stable.

06:21.820 --> 06:22.020
Right.

06:22.360 --> 06:24.280
It is a state of total stability.

06:24.900 --> 06:27.540
The calculation has no pending obligations.

06:28.100 --> 06:29.840
It has paid all its debts.

06:30.260 --> 06:30.940
It is finished.

06:31.020 --> 06:31.760
Okay, I love that.

06:31.920 --> 06:33.400
It has paid all its debts.

06:33.400 --> 06:37.980
And here is where affliction makes the pivotal connection to our everyday lives.

06:38.840 --> 06:46.000
When a programmer creates an abstraction, say, they write a complex function for a video game that calculates gravity.

06:46.120 --> 06:46.240
Right.

06:46.320 --> 06:47.800
They name it gravity math or something.

06:47.880 --> 06:48.320
Exactly.

06:48.540 --> 06:52.020
And then they just use that name elsewhere in the code without looking inside it.

06:52.020 --> 06:54.180
They are not just putting a rug over a mess.

06:54.260 --> 06:54.980
They are hiding it.

06:55.100 --> 07:00.560
No, they are implicitly trusting that the internal calculation, the nested logic, has already been reduced.

07:00.820 --> 07:02.980
They are treating it as a normal form.

07:03.120 --> 07:06.980
This is great because it completely shifts how we look at a black box.

07:06.980 --> 07:10.000
Usually we think of a black box as something opaque.

07:10.200 --> 07:11.160
Right, like a mystery.

07:11.560 --> 07:13.840
Yeah, we say, oh, I don't know how my car engine works.

07:13.880 --> 07:14.720
It's just a black box.

07:14.800 --> 07:16.040
I'm ignorant of the details.

07:16.620 --> 07:19.640
But affliction is right and abstraction is evaluation.

07:20.020 --> 07:22.640
Then when we look at a black box, we aren't being ignorant.

07:22.960 --> 07:23.560
Not at all.

07:23.740 --> 07:27.640
We are acknowledging that the work inside that box is finished.

07:28.040 --> 07:34.220
The surface of the box, like the steering wheel, the gas pedal, is only available to us because the depth has been executed.

07:34.220 --> 07:35.360
That's a great way to put it.

07:35.440 --> 07:39.000
The abstraction is the certificate of a completed computation.

07:39.520 --> 07:41.380
It is the proof that the work is done.

07:41.620 --> 07:45.040
Let me try an analogy here to see if I'm fully grasping the mechanics.

07:45.420 --> 07:45.740
Yeah.

07:45.920 --> 07:47.180
Think about filing your taxes.

07:47.400 --> 07:48.860
Okay, painful, but go on.

07:49.240 --> 07:54.720
When you do your taxes, you have to do all this nested, incredibly tedious arithmetic.

07:55.260 --> 08:03.300
You're adding up individual receipts, calculating specific deductions, carrying numbers from Schedule C to Schedule 1 to Form 1040.

08:03.300 --> 08:05.680
It is a total nightmare of dependencies.

08:06.020 --> 08:06.140
Right.

08:06.380 --> 08:08.400
If line 4 changes, line 12 changes.

08:08.840 --> 08:11.980
But at the very end of all that suffering, you get a single number.

08:12.380 --> 08:14.380
You owe this much or you get this much back.

08:14.640 --> 08:18.740
You don't hand the government a shoebox full of all your receipts and your messy scratch paper.

08:19.080 --> 08:21.660
You just hand them the final reduced number.

08:21.940 --> 08:22.380
Exactly.

08:22.760 --> 08:24.740
The messy work isn't hidden from the IRS.

08:25.380 --> 08:26.660
It's simply finished.

08:27.160 --> 08:32.520
That is an absolutely brilliant analogy, and it perfectly maps onto Affliction's argument.

08:32.520 --> 08:36.180
Handing over that final number is an act of abstraction.

08:36.440 --> 08:38.980
You are presenting the government with a normal form.

08:39.300 --> 08:41.500
And they don't need the receipts because the math is done.

08:41.740 --> 08:41.940
Right.

08:42.160 --> 08:48.020
The government interacts with that abstract value because they don't need to re-evaluate the inner redexes,

08:48.380 --> 08:52.580
the individual grocery receipts or the gas mileage logs.

08:53.060 --> 08:54.620
The computation has stabilized.

08:55.100 --> 08:57.160
The complexity wasn't hidden under a rug.

08:57.260 --> 08:58.880
It was mathematically exhausted.

08:58.880 --> 08:59.580
It was.

09:00.000 --> 09:03.440
And Affliction takes this even further by pointing to something called church encodings.

09:03.860 --> 09:08.920
Remember a moment ago when you were shocked that lambda calculus doesn't have native data types,

09:08.980 --> 09:10.940
not even numbers or true and false?

09:11.080 --> 09:11.440
Yes.

09:11.780 --> 09:12.760
I'm still stuck on that.

09:13.020 --> 09:15.640
If you don't have true or false, how can you write logic?

09:16.280 --> 09:17.500
How does a computer know what to do?

09:17.760 --> 09:18.620
Through behavior.

09:19.160 --> 09:24.380
In church encodings, a concept like true is defined purely procedurally.

09:24.660 --> 09:26.560
It isn't a magical inherent property.

09:26.560 --> 09:27.080
Okay.

09:27.220 --> 09:28.000
So what is it?

09:28.320 --> 09:34.260
True is constructed as a lambda term, a function that takes two arguments and always returns the first one.

09:34.660 --> 09:39.420
By contrast, false is a function that takes two arguments and always returns the second one.

09:39.540 --> 09:40.840
Wait, let me visualize that.

09:40.940 --> 09:48.100
So true is basically like a bouncer at a club who is handed two IDs and his only job,

09:48.220 --> 09:51.600
his only defining characteristic is that he always hands back the first ID.

09:51.740 --> 09:52.000
Right.

09:52.340 --> 09:55.560
And false is a bouncer who always hands back the second ID.

09:55.560 --> 09:57.400
That's a fun way to picture it, yes.

09:58.060 --> 10:00.800
Truth is defined entirely by how it navigates a choice.

10:01.220 --> 10:03.800
It contains no primitive substance of truthiness.

10:04.060 --> 10:06.060
There's no magical truth particle inside it.

10:06.160 --> 10:09.200
No elemental particle of truth inside the function at all.

10:09.580 --> 10:14.200
But, and this is the key, once you write that lambda term and it reduces to normal form,

10:14.380 --> 10:17.260
it behaves exactly like an abstract Boolean value.

10:17.500 --> 10:17.680
Oh, wow.

10:17.940 --> 10:20.340
You don't need to look inside the bouncer's head anymore.

10:20.440 --> 10:23.220
The underlying mechanism collapses into a stable behavior.

10:23.220 --> 10:26.580
So the meaning of the term is entirely defined by what it does.

10:26.920 --> 10:27.200
Yes.

10:27.520 --> 10:29.740
The text argues that this isn't just a metaphor.

10:30.000 --> 10:32.380
It is a literal epistemic compression.

10:33.180 --> 10:38.960
The abstract idea of truth is just the stabilized execution of a choosing mechanism.

10:39.380 --> 10:39.600
Okay.

10:39.660 --> 10:42.920
This brings up something from the reading that I really wanted you to break down for me.

10:43.440 --> 10:46.720
The text mentions something called the Curry-Howard correspondence.

10:47.140 --> 10:48.060
Oh, yes.

10:48.060 --> 10:51.660
And it ties this all back to how humans actually prove things to be true.

10:51.960 --> 10:52.480
What is that?

10:52.620 --> 10:55.960
The Curry-Howard correspondence is considered one of the most profound,

10:56.180 --> 10:59.760
almost magical discoveries in the history of computer science and logic.

11:00.000 --> 11:00.560
Big words.

11:00.740 --> 11:01.300
What does it do?

11:01.400 --> 11:06.300
It proves that there is a direct, undeniable mathematical isomorphism,

11:06.560 --> 11:11.420
a perfect structural mirror between computer programs and logical proofs.

11:11.420 --> 11:15.720
Meaning, what exactly, that writing code is the same as doing philosophy.

11:15.900 --> 11:16.800
Basically, yes.

11:17.080 --> 11:21.820
It means that the type of a function in a program corresponds exactly to a logical proposition.

11:22.320 --> 11:27.520
And the actual code written inside that function, the implementation, the step-by-step instructions,

11:28.220 --> 11:31.120
corresponds to the mathematical proof of that proposition.

11:31.620 --> 11:33.680
Programs are proofs, and proofs are programs.

11:33.780 --> 11:36.620
They are the exact same object viewed through two different lenses.

11:36.620 --> 11:42.440
So, if I sit down at my keyboard and I write a software program that successfully takes an input of type A

11:42.440 --> 11:47.080
and returns an output of type B without crashing, I haven't just built a tool.

11:47.480 --> 11:53.120
I have literally written a mathematical proof that proposition A implies proposition B.

11:53.180 --> 11:53.740
You have.

11:54.120 --> 11:57.420
And under this Curry-Howard correspondence, Fliction asks,

11:58.020 --> 12:00.600
What does it mean to evaluate a program?

12:00.600 --> 12:07.720
If running a program is beta reduction, what is the equivalent action in the realm of logical proofs?

12:07.820 --> 12:10.520
Wait, do proofs have, like, running code?

12:11.320 --> 12:13.300
I thought a proof was just a static document.

12:13.400 --> 12:14.000
Not exactly.

12:14.100 --> 12:17.860
In formal logic, when you construct a proof, it often takes meandering detours.

12:18.080 --> 12:19.800
Like getting lost on the way to the conclusion.

12:20.060 --> 12:20.340
Right.

12:20.660 --> 12:23.720
You might prove a tiny sub-point, an intermediate lima,

12:23.780 --> 12:26.360
that you end up not really needing for the final conclusion,

12:26.680 --> 12:29.280
or you might take the long way around a logical problem.

12:29.280 --> 12:32.080
In logic, these detours are called cuts.

12:32.700 --> 12:36.580
There is a famous theorem by Gerhard Gensen, the cut elimination theorem,

12:36.720 --> 12:40.320
which shows that you can methodically eliminate all of these logical detours

12:40.320 --> 12:45.480
to get a direct, completely streamlined, normalized proof.

12:45.680 --> 12:46.900
Oh, I see where this is going.

12:47.140 --> 12:47.680
Let me guess.

12:48.480 --> 12:51.500
Eliminating cuts in a philosophical logic proof

12:51.500 --> 12:56.780
is the exact same mathematical mechanism as beta reduction in lambda calculus.

12:56.940 --> 12:58.400
It is structurally identical.

12:58.400 --> 13:03.180
Removing a logical detour is exactly the same as evaluating an inner redex.

13:03.500 --> 13:03.600
Wow.

13:04.200 --> 13:07.640
So, Fogutian concludes that to abstract is to finish a proof.

13:08.300 --> 13:12.520
When you use an abstraction, you are relying on the fact that all the logical detours,

13:12.860 --> 13:16.420
all the messy intermediate steps, have been evaluated away.

13:16.820 --> 13:20.020
You are left with a concise, stable certificate of truth.

13:20.300 --> 13:21.820
That is genuinely beautiful.

13:22.020 --> 13:23.900
Abstraction isn't running away from the details.

13:24.020 --> 13:24.880
It's conquering them.

13:24.880 --> 13:28.860
It's wrestling the math into submission until it stops demanding your attention.

13:29.000 --> 13:29.800
Conquering the details.

13:29.920 --> 13:31.920
Yes, that captures the spirit of the text perfectly.

13:32.080 --> 13:34.320
Okay, so this lays an incredible foundation.

13:35.140 --> 13:38.200
We understand that abstraction is the completion of internal work.

13:38.540 --> 13:40.980
But that naturally begs a massive question.

13:41.220 --> 13:45.740
We don't live in a world of pure mathematical lambda expressions floating in the void.

13:45.740 --> 13:48.020
We live in a world of things.

13:48.680 --> 13:52.780
We deal with objects, structures, interfaces, tangible entities.

13:53.040 --> 13:53.160
Right.

13:53.380 --> 13:54.200
The real world.

13:54.300 --> 14:00.620
How does Fliction apply this radical reframing of math to the actual structure of the everyday world?

14:00.780 --> 14:05.300
This is where the text moves from the operational semantics of code into ontology.

14:06.100 --> 14:13.480
And ontology, for our listener, is the philosophical study of being what it means for a thing to exist.

14:13.600 --> 14:14.880
The heavy philosophy stuff.

14:14.880 --> 14:15.480
Exactly.

14:15.800 --> 14:21.320
To make this leap, Fliction looks at modern functional programming languages like Haskell or Racket.

14:21.920 --> 14:25.700
In these languages, you don't define what a chunk of data is internally.

14:26.360 --> 14:29.340
Instead, you define its interactions through a type signature.

14:29.500 --> 14:30.300
I've seen a little bit of this.

14:30.360 --> 14:35.440
Like, you write a function and you explicitly declare this function takes a number and it gives back a string of text.

14:35.820 --> 14:37.320
You define the inputs and outputs.

14:37.580 --> 14:41.340
Yes, but Fliction takes it much deeper into how we define complex entities.

14:42.040 --> 14:44.120
Consider a mathematical structure like a ring.

14:44.120 --> 14:45.240
Okay, like from algebra.

14:45.620 --> 14:45.900
Right.

14:46.180 --> 14:53.260
In a language like Haskell, you don't define a ring by describing its physical substance or how its data is stored in memory.

14:53.480 --> 14:55.680
You define it purely by its affordances.

14:56.160 --> 14:56.720
Affordances.

14:56.720 --> 14:58.700
Let's make sure that's crystal clear for the listener.

14:58.700 --> 15:01.520
What exactly is an affordance in this context?

15:02.020 --> 15:05.020
An affordance is simply what an entity permits you to do with it.

15:05.260 --> 15:06.740
It's the potential for interaction.

15:07.540 --> 15:08.700
Think of a physical chair.

15:08.900 --> 15:09.140
Okay.

15:09.140 --> 15:14.360
You don't define a chair by its atomic structure or whether it's made of wood or plastic or steel.

15:14.720 --> 15:17.220
You define it by the fact that it affords sitting.

15:17.780 --> 15:19.900
It permits a human body to rest on it.

15:20.080 --> 15:20.700
That makes sense.

15:20.920 --> 15:27.580
In Haskell, a ring is an interface that requires its participants to afford three very specific operations.

15:28.320 --> 15:30.960
Addition, multiplication, and negation.

15:30.960 --> 15:35.960
So if a piece of data can be added, multiplied, and negated, it is a ring.

15:36.620 --> 15:45.700
It literally doesn't matter if, under the hood, that data is a simple integer or a complex fraction or a massive matrix of numbers.

15:45.820 --> 15:46.260
Exactly.

15:46.440 --> 15:48.860
If it affords the behaviors, it earns the title.

15:49.200 --> 15:49.720
Precisely.

15:49.900 --> 15:50.060
Yeah.

15:50.140 --> 15:52.400
And this is a massive ontological shift.

15:52.760 --> 15:56.440
A type signature doesn't describe what a substance is on the inside.

15:56.580 --> 15:59.000
It dictates what roles it can play on the outside.

15:59.000 --> 16:01.940
And this leads to a pretty big critique in the text, right?

16:02.220 --> 16:02.480
Yes.

16:02.880 --> 16:12.900
Fliction uses this perspective to launch a truly fascinating and probably quite controversial critique of object-oriented programming, or OOP.

16:13.200 --> 16:13.740
Oh, boy.

16:14.280 --> 16:21.460
I know enough about software engineering to know that the programmers listening are either going to cheer enthusiastically or throw their phones across the room.

16:21.580 --> 16:22.720
It's a heated topic.

16:23.040 --> 16:26.720
What is Fliction's beef with object-oriented programming?

16:26.720 --> 16:28.780
I mean, it runs most of the modern internet.

16:29.180 --> 16:33.020
It does, but Fliction argues it is philosophically flawed at its core.

16:33.620 --> 16:38.460
In traditional object-oriented programming, you build structures using inheritance hierarchies.

16:38.540 --> 16:38.700
Right.

16:38.800 --> 16:39.860
Parent and child classes.

16:39.960 --> 16:40.260
Exactly.

16:40.440 --> 16:44.760
You create a parent class, let's say, animal, and then you create a child class, dog.

16:45.300 --> 16:53.140
The way OOP works is that the dog automatically inherits all the properties, the data structures, and the historical details of the animal class.

16:53.140 --> 16:53.660
Right.

16:53.840 --> 16:55.320
It inherits the internal state.

16:55.460 --> 16:57.600
It brings all the baggage of the parent along with it.

16:57.660 --> 16:58.060
Exactly.

16:58.540 --> 17:04.380
And Fliction argues that this inheritance forces objects to carry around irrelevant historical details.

17:04.720 --> 17:05.260
How so?

17:05.260 --> 17:08.860
Let's say you are building a system and you just need an object that can bark.

17:09.400 --> 17:11.300
That's the only affordance you care about.

17:11.740 --> 17:21.920
But because you inherit it from animal, your dog object is suddenly forced to carry around the metabolic rate, the skeletal structure, and the evolutionary history of the parent class.

17:21.920 --> 17:23.840
Even if you never use any of that.

17:23.920 --> 17:24.240
Right.

17:24.340 --> 17:27.400
Even if your specific program will never, ever use them.

17:28.180 --> 17:31.540
Fliction calls this the reification of irrelevant detail.

17:31.760 --> 17:34.660
It proliferates obligations rather than reducing them.

17:34.960 --> 17:39.000
There is a really famous quote in programming that perfectly encapsulates this.

17:39.380 --> 17:40.760
I think it was Joe Armstrong who said,

17:41.300 --> 17:46.740
You wanted a banana, but what you got was a gorilla holding the banana and the entire jungle.

17:47.200 --> 17:50.540
That is the exact dynamic Fliction is critiquing.

17:50.540 --> 17:54.760
The gorilla and the jungle represent extractive, hoarding design.

17:55.240 --> 17:57.700
You are hoarding context you simply don't need.

17:57.920 --> 17:59.600
And functional programming is different.

17:59.860 --> 18:07.180
In contrast, Fliction argues that interface-based design, like what we see in functional programming, is ontologically pure.

18:07.900 --> 18:10.960
An interface simply walks up to an entity and asks,

18:11.860 --> 18:13.480
Do you have a method for barking?

18:14.100 --> 18:15.820
It just checks the ID at the door.

18:15.880 --> 18:16.140
Right.

18:16.240 --> 18:17.680
It doesn't care about your ancestry.

18:17.680 --> 18:22.560
It doesn't care if you inherited from animal or if you are a robot dog built from scratch.

18:22.640 --> 18:27.980
It models true abstraction because it rigorously excludes all incidental detail.

18:28.280 --> 18:31.440
Let me push back on this a little because Fliction is making a really aggressive claim here.

18:31.800 --> 18:40.280
Is the text seriously asserting that object-oriented programming is morally or philosophically flawed because it hoards details,

18:40.560 --> 18:45.180
while functional programming is somehow ontologically superior because it defines boundaries?

18:45.180 --> 18:46.920
It is a bold claim, but yes.

18:47.020 --> 18:50.040
That's a massive philosophical weight to put on how we organize code.

18:50.200 --> 18:52.280
The text is absolutely making that claim.

18:52.520 --> 18:56.060
Fliction asserts that an entity is what it affords, not what it descends from.

18:56.780 --> 19:01.140
The being of a thing is determined entirely by the roles it occupies in a system.

19:01.140 --> 19:06.640
So inheritance looks backward at history, while interfaces look forward at potential action.

19:06.860 --> 19:07.520
Beautifully said.

19:07.900 --> 19:15.900
And this brings us to the ultimate mathematical expression of this idea, which Fliction spends a lot of time on, category theory.

19:16.200 --> 19:19.780
I've heard category theory described as the mathematics of mathematics.

19:20.520 --> 19:21.660
It's incredibly dense.

19:21.840 --> 19:22.860
How does it fit into this?

19:23.060 --> 19:26.640
It sounds intimidating, but the core visual is quite intuitive.

19:27.100 --> 19:30.540
In category theory, you have objects and you have morphisms.

19:30.540 --> 19:31.140
Morphisms?

19:31.400 --> 19:36.240
Morphisms are just the arrows connecting the objects representing transformations or relationships.

19:36.600 --> 19:44.020
But the radical mind-bending part of category theory is that the objects themselves are completely featureless.

19:44.280 --> 19:44.780
Featureless?

19:44.840 --> 19:46.080
What do you mean, like blank circles?

19:46.240 --> 19:46.520
Yes.

19:46.780 --> 19:48.080
They have no internal contents.

19:48.440 --> 19:52.220
You are mathematically forbidden from looking inside an object in category theory.

19:52.440 --> 19:56.920
They are defined entirely by the network of arrow, the morphisms in which they participate.

19:56.920 --> 20:11.440
So to use our earlier example, if I have a featureless dot on a whiteboard and there is an arrow pointing to it that says feeds on breadcrumbs and an arrow pointing away from it that says produces a quack, I don't need to cut the dot open to see if it has feathers or a beak.

20:11.980 --> 20:14.680
The incoming and outgoing arrows are the duck.

20:14.680 --> 20:16.200
That is exactly right.

20:16.540 --> 20:19.440
The identity of an object is exhausted by its morphisms.

20:19.980 --> 20:23.580
Infliction connects us back to the idea of abstraction as reduction.

20:24.240 --> 20:25.340
How does that connect to the essays?

20:25.720 --> 20:30.760
The text uses a concept called verified domains from one of the supplemental essays.

20:31.660 --> 20:37.980
An interface in software or a morphism in category theory establishes a verified boundary.

20:38.400 --> 20:39.680
It is a strict contract.

20:39.820 --> 20:40.680
Like a legal agreement?

20:40.680 --> 20:48.060
Right. It says, I promise I will not look at your internal implementation and in exchange you guarantee this specific interaction.

20:48.600 --> 20:54.500
The abstraction is the reduction of complex, messy objects down to the pure invariance of their participation.

20:54.780 --> 20:56.540
The invariance of their participation.

20:57.160 --> 20:58.800
That is a beautiful way to phrase it.

20:59.080 --> 21:00.600
You are what you reliably do.

21:01.220 --> 21:02.680
Okay, I follow the logic.

21:03.100 --> 21:08.440
It works beautifully in the pristine, flawless, mathematical world of code and category theory.

21:08.840 --> 21:10.140
But here is where I hit a wall.

21:10.140 --> 21:10.740
Where is the wall?

21:10.960 --> 21:12.140
We don't just live in math.

21:12.800 --> 21:14.880
Code eventually has to run on physical machines.

21:15.260 --> 21:16.780
It lives on silicon wafers.

21:16.920 --> 21:17.780
It runs on electricity.

21:18.100 --> 21:19.640
It generates physical heat.

21:20.320 --> 21:20.980
Very true.

21:21.300 --> 21:26.080
And physics doesn't care about our neat little software interfaces or featureless categorical dots.

21:26.740 --> 21:30.920
If abstraction is fundamentally about reduction and completing a task,

21:30.920 --> 21:38.340
how does a literal, messy electron in a physical wire know that a computation is finished?

21:38.660 --> 21:41.100
How does physical hardware abstract anything?

21:41.100 --> 21:44.400
This is exactly where Fliction's theory gets put to the test,

21:44.820 --> 21:48.560
moving from abstract logic to the muriology of the physical world.

21:49.040 --> 21:49.580
Muriology.

21:49.820 --> 21:50.940
The study of parts and holes, right?

21:50.940 --> 21:51.360
Exactly.

21:51.900 --> 21:54.140
And to understand how physical abstraction works,

21:54.420 --> 22:00.900
Fliction points us toward a very specific type of microchip architecture called null convention logic, or NCL.

22:01.000 --> 22:04.380
Okay, let's break down how a normal microchip works first so we can see the contrast.

22:04.540 --> 22:04.740
Yeah.

22:04.740 --> 22:08.420
Most computers sitting on our desks or in our pockets right now are synchronous, right?

22:08.440 --> 22:08.840
Yes, they are.

22:08.900 --> 22:09.580
They run on a clock.

22:09.920 --> 22:10.680
Tick-tock, tick-tock.

22:10.960 --> 22:12.180
Billions of times a second.

22:12.360 --> 22:12.740
Correct.

22:13.180 --> 22:15.100
You buy a 3 gigahertz processor.

22:15.480 --> 22:20.000
That means there is a microscopic metronome ticking 3 billion times a second.

22:20.680 --> 22:24.660
That global clock enforces the timing of all computations.

22:25.340 --> 22:26.700
So everyone has to keep pace.

22:26.880 --> 22:32.060
Every logic gate, every transistor on the chip marches to the exact same drumbeat.

22:32.060 --> 22:37.460
When the clock ticks, everyone passes their data to the next stage, ready or not.

22:37.720 --> 22:43.880
Which means you have to design the chip so that the slowest piece of math finishes before the clock ticks, otherwise you get a crash.

22:44.100 --> 22:44.400
Yes.

22:44.760 --> 22:47.100
It requires massive overhead to synchronize.

22:47.660 --> 22:50.320
But NCL is an asynchronous hardware architecture.

22:50.620 --> 22:51.840
There is no global clock.

22:52.060 --> 22:52.320
None.

22:52.420 --> 22:53.400
Wait, no clock at all?

22:53.540 --> 22:53.820
None.

22:54.060 --> 22:56.780
The parts of the chip compute at their own individual speeds,

22:56.940 --> 23:00.360
and they only pass data along when they are fully finished with their specific task.

23:00.360 --> 23:01.140
But wait.

23:02.060 --> 23:08.840
If there's no clock acting as a conductor, how does the next part of the circuit know when the data is actually ready?

23:09.380 --> 23:12.120
How does stage B know stage A is finished?

23:12.240 --> 23:14.720
They communicate through what's called dual rail encoding.

23:15.100 --> 23:18.220
In a standard computer, a single wire represents a bit of data.

23:18.600 --> 23:20.980
If there is high voltage on the wire, it's a 1.

23:21.220 --> 23:23.100
If there is low voltage, it's a 0.

23:23.400 --> 23:24.200
Okay, pretty standard.

23:24.200 --> 23:29.500
But in NCL, a single bit of data is represented by two physical wires side-by-side.

23:29.700 --> 23:32.040
Let's call them rail 0 and rail 1.

23:32.180 --> 23:32.460
Okay.

23:32.920 --> 23:34.160
Two wires for one bit.

23:34.260 --> 23:34.880
How does that help?

23:35.260 --> 23:41.360
If the circuit finishes its math and the answer is true, it sends high voltage down rail 1.

23:42.020 --> 23:45.980
If the answer is false, it sends high voltage down rail 0.

23:45.980 --> 23:47.540
And what if it's still doing the math?

23:47.680 --> 23:49.860
What if neither rail has high voltage yet?

23:50.000 --> 23:52.140
That is the absolute genius of NCL.

23:52.640 --> 23:59.220
If neither rail is asserted, if both are low, the signal is in an incomplete or N-E-L-L state.

23:59.380 --> 24:04.420
It's literally holding up a sign that says, hold on, I'm still thinking, don't look at me yet.

24:04.420 --> 24:05.100
Exactly.

24:05.740 --> 24:08.740
Infliction makes a profound ontological point here.

24:09.440 --> 24:11.940
In this system, the incomplete state is not an error.

24:12.160 --> 24:12.960
It is not a glitch.

24:13.080 --> 24:15.700
It is a legitimate physical mode of being.

24:15.800 --> 24:17.100
It's just a state of processing.

24:17.360 --> 24:17.600
Right.

24:17.820 --> 24:20.060
It represents ongoing physical computation.

24:20.600 --> 24:25.640
The signal must physically stabilize, meaning one of the two rails must definitively assert itself

24:25.640 --> 24:29.260
before it can be treated as a black box output for the next stage of the circuit.

24:29.340 --> 24:30.800
Oh, I see it.

24:30.860 --> 24:31.860
That's like baking a cake.

24:31.860 --> 24:34.540
If I have a bowl of batter, it's full of potential.

24:34.800 --> 24:38.240
It has all the ingredients, but I can't interact with it like a cake.

24:38.340 --> 24:39.160
I can't frost it.

24:39.240 --> 24:40.140
I can't slice it.

24:40.400 --> 24:43.420
I can't stack it on top of another cake to make tears.

24:43.740 --> 24:43.860
Right.

24:44.040 --> 24:46.160
It has to physically stabilize in the oven first.

24:46.420 --> 24:49.020
It has to undergo a phase change into a cake.

24:50.000 --> 24:55.040
Is this what the compressed causality excerpt means when it talks about a stability sequence?

24:55.300 --> 24:57.460
That cake analogy is spot on, yes.

24:57.780 --> 25:00.140
The batter is the dual rail N-E-L-L state.

25:00.140 --> 25:03.220
It is structurally useless to the next stage of the recipe.

25:03.740 --> 25:03.760
Right.

25:03.920 --> 25:04.980
You can't build with batter.

25:05.300 --> 25:10.620
The abstraction in hardware is precisely the transition from incomplete informational potential,

25:10.880 --> 25:15.420
the batter, to a determinate, composable output, the baked cake.

25:15.900 --> 25:19.760
Once the physical voltage pattern collapses into a definitive value on one of those rails,

25:20.120 --> 25:24.920
the inner microscopic timing behaviors, the fluctuations that produced it, no longer matter.

25:25.120 --> 25:26.880
They have been abstracted away.

25:26.880 --> 25:31.780
Because the computation is finished, the cake is baked, the math is done, the next circuit

25:31.780 --> 25:33.460
can just grab the results and run with it.

25:33.720 --> 25:34.140
Exactly.

25:34.720 --> 25:40.340
The physical stabilization of the hardware is the exact tangible equivalent to beta reduction

25:40.340 --> 25:41.540
in the lambda calculus.

25:42.100 --> 25:43.680
It is the completion of the obligation.

25:44.640 --> 25:48.560
And Fliction uses this to prove that abstraction is substrate independent.

25:48.860 --> 25:52.420
Meaning it doesn't matter what physical material you use to do the math.

25:52.420 --> 25:52.860
Right.

25:52.980 --> 25:57.200
It doesn't matter if the logic is implemented in silicon pathways or fiber optic pulses

25:57.200 --> 26:02.820
of light or pneumatic fluidic valves or even, as Fliction cheekily suggests in a footnote,

26:03.280 --> 26:06.080
a wet sand mold vibrating at 60 hertz.

26:06.260 --> 26:07.020
Wet sand, really?

26:07.240 --> 26:07.460
Yeah.

26:07.640 --> 26:12.440
The invariant principle of the universe is that incomplete computations must resolve before

26:12.440 --> 26:14.380
higher order behavior becomes meaningful.

26:14.580 --> 26:16.780
And this connects deeply back to muriology, right?

26:17.100 --> 26:19.840
How do smaller things combine to create bigger things?

26:19.840 --> 26:20.220
Mm-hmm.

26:20.280 --> 26:25.320
Sliction argues that to abstract is to make a muriological move, to ascend from the messy

26:25.320 --> 26:27.080
parts to the unified whole.

26:27.180 --> 26:27.500
Yes.

26:27.720 --> 26:32.160
In muriology, we talk about fusion, the merging of parts into a minimal hole that contains

26:32.160 --> 26:32.460
them.

26:32.940 --> 26:37.460
When those NCL wires are fluctuating in the NUL state, they are parts in flux.

26:37.460 --> 26:42.060
But the moment they stabilize and assert a value, they fuse into a hole, a determinate,

26:42.220 --> 26:43.580
readable, Boolean value.

26:44.020 --> 26:48.760
And once it fuses into a hole, the next stage of the circuit can treat it as a single,

26:48.760 --> 26:50.960
simple part for an even bigger hole.

26:51.500 --> 26:52.100
It scales up.

26:52.340 --> 26:52.780
Precisely.

26:52.900 --> 26:54.100
It scales infinitely.

26:54.400 --> 26:54.820
Mm-hmm.

26:54.820 --> 27:00.740
But, and this is a massive but, this scaling relies entirely on the successful execution

27:00.740 --> 27:02.080
of the stability sequence.

27:02.520 --> 27:07.940
If the intercomputation doesn't stabilize, say the oven breaks and the batter never bakes,

27:08.380 --> 27:09.440
what happens?

27:09.500 --> 27:10.340
Extraction fails.

27:10.440 --> 27:11.660
You can't frost batter.

27:11.740 --> 27:12.040
Right.

27:12.040 --> 27:15.880
If the circuit gets stuck in the NEL state, the abstraction fails.

27:16.300 --> 27:21.560
The compressed causality text refers to this specifically as stability failure as structural

27:21.560 --> 27:22.020
result.

27:22.740 --> 27:26.060
You cannot build a muriological hole on unstable parts.

27:26.420 --> 27:30.660
So if the foundation hasn't successfully evaluated into normal form, the entire higher

27:30.660 --> 27:31.880
order structure collapses.

27:32.160 --> 27:32.620
Exactly.

27:32.860 --> 27:33.080
Wow.

27:33.240 --> 27:33.380
Okay.

27:33.380 --> 27:38.060
So if physical circuits abstract by stabilizing into predictable outputs, and this is a universal

27:38.060 --> 27:42.640
rule, what happens when we look at the most complex, messy, biological, physical circuit

27:42.640 --> 27:43.160
in existence?

27:43.360 --> 27:44.200
You mean the human brain?

27:44.340 --> 27:45.380
I am talking about the human brain.

27:45.600 --> 27:48.380
How does this universal rule of abstraction apply to us?

27:48.500 --> 27:50.780
Are we just walking computers trying to read normal form?

27:51.040 --> 27:56.000
This is where fliction takes the theory from hardware into cognitive science, specifically

27:56.000 --> 28:00.820
relying on a very prominent modern neurological framework called predictive coding.

28:00.820 --> 28:01.260
Okay.

28:01.600 --> 28:04.020
Let's unpack predictive coding because this is fascinating.

28:04.780 --> 28:10.280
The old classical way of thinking about the brain was that it's basically a passive receiver,

28:10.500 --> 28:10.600
right?

28:10.720 --> 28:10.900
Correct.

28:11.040 --> 28:15.800
Your eyes take in light, your ears take in sound, that raw data travels up your nerves,

28:15.920 --> 28:21.440
and the brain sits back in its dark skull theater and processes the data to figure out what's

28:21.440 --> 28:22.300
going on outside.

28:22.500 --> 28:22.760
Right.

28:22.920 --> 28:25.880
That is known as the bottom-up model of perception.

28:26.120 --> 28:28.540
The brain waits for the world to tell it what is happening.

28:28.540 --> 28:31.500
But predictive coding flips that entirely upside down.

28:31.620 --> 28:31.960
How so?

28:32.200 --> 28:35.860
It argues that the brain is fundamentally a proactive prediction engine.

28:36.400 --> 28:40.640
It is constantly constructing hierarchical generative models of the world.

28:41.000 --> 28:42.220
It doesn't wait for data.

28:42.600 --> 28:48.060
It projects predictions downward to the sensory organs, guessing what it is about to see, hear,

28:48.200 --> 28:50.760
and feel a fraction of a second before it happens.

28:50.940 --> 28:52.660
I think I need a concrete example of this.

28:53.160 --> 28:56.300
How does projecting a prediction change how I experience the world?

28:56.300 --> 28:59.540
Think about walking down a familiar flight of stairs in the dark.

29:00.380 --> 29:04.680
Your brain isn't waiting for your foot to hit the step to know where the step is.

29:04.920 --> 29:05.200
True.

29:05.300 --> 29:06.840
I just sort of walk on autopilot.

29:07.080 --> 29:12.980
Because it has a generative model of your staircase, it predicts exactly when your foot will hit

29:12.980 --> 29:16.020
the wood, exactly how much pressure to expect.

29:16.420 --> 29:21.100
You are essentially hallucinating the staircase based on memory and physics.

29:21.100 --> 29:23.120
Okay, so what happens when I miss a step?

29:23.600 --> 29:27.560
We've all had that horrible stomach-dropping feeling when you think there's one more step,

29:27.700 --> 29:30.360
but you're actually at the bottom and your foot just slams into the floor.

29:30.560 --> 29:34.040
That stomach-dropping feeling is exactly how the brain processes data.

29:34.800 --> 29:39.600
In predictive coding, the sensory input coming up from your foot isn't used to build the image

29:39.600 --> 29:40.200
of the floor.

29:40.580 --> 29:41.160
Wait, it's not.

29:41.520 --> 29:44.380
No, it is only used to calculate prediction errors.

29:44.380 --> 29:49.720
The mismatch between what your brain expected, another step, and what actually happened,

29:50.080 --> 29:50.940
the solid floor.

29:51.520 --> 29:55.680
Your brain's ultimate overarching goal is to minimize the surprise.

29:56.320 --> 29:58.320
It wants to make prediction errors go away.

29:58.820 --> 30:01.560
It wants the physical world to match its internal model perfectly.

30:01.920 --> 30:07.240
Yes, and fliction maps this neurological process onto a profound geometric space.

30:07.680 --> 30:11.400
The text describes human belief as a geometric manifold.

30:11.700 --> 30:12.820
A geometric manifold.

30:12.940 --> 30:13.700
Let's visualize that.

30:13.700 --> 30:16.920
A manifold is like a multidimensional surface, right?

30:17.120 --> 30:19.720
Like a trampoline or a landscape with hills and valleys.

30:19.900 --> 30:20.120
Yes.

30:20.480 --> 30:23.080
Imagine a complex, multidimensional landscape.

30:23.880 --> 30:27.500
In this model, your beliefs, your expectations about the world,

30:27.840 --> 30:29.640
are points resting on that landscape.

30:29.740 --> 30:29.960
Okay.

30:30.220 --> 30:33.220
When you experience surprise, when you miss that step in the dark,

30:33.680 --> 30:36.120
it isn't just a statistical error in your neurons.

30:36.540 --> 30:37.980
Fliction defines it geometrically.

30:38.500 --> 30:40.500
Surprise is a curvature signal.

30:40.720 --> 30:41.500
Curvature signal.

30:41.500 --> 30:45.640
It indicates a geometric mismatch between the tangent space of your belief manifold

30:45.640 --> 30:48.820
and the actual causal structure of the physical world.

30:48.820 --> 30:52.860
So when I am surprised, my brain is physically, or at least geometrically,

30:53.340 --> 30:56.540
feeling the curvature of reality pushing back against my assumptions.

30:57.000 --> 30:58.300
The trampoline is warped.

30:58.420 --> 31:00.000
That is a perfect visualization.

31:00.280 --> 31:01.600
The mythmatch creates tension.

31:01.940 --> 31:05.180
It forces your cognitive system to flow down the gradient of free energy,

31:05.640 --> 31:07.660
updating your belief to flatten out that curvature.

31:07.660 --> 31:11.300
Ah, so you realize you are at the bottom of the stairs,

31:11.660 --> 31:14.800
the surprise vanishes, and your model is realigned.

31:14.980 --> 31:15.340
Exactly.

31:15.740 --> 31:17.720
This is what we call learning, or inference.

31:18.520 --> 31:23.760
The continuous deformation of your belief manifold to compress the causal structure

31:23.760 --> 31:26.720
of the external world into a manageable shape.

31:26.900 --> 31:31.060
Okay, I follow the geometry, but I have a major philosophical problem here.

31:31.540 --> 31:33.360
Where do I fit into this trampoline?

31:33.700 --> 31:36.360
Where is the self in this predictive model?

31:36.360 --> 31:37.340
The great question.

31:37.500 --> 31:40.380
If I am just a geometry problem trying to flatten out surprise,

31:41.520 --> 31:42.740
what is my personality?

31:43.460 --> 31:47.680
Here is where Fluxian makes a profound ontological claim about human identity.

31:48.460 --> 31:51.080
The text argues that the self is not an object.

31:51.400 --> 31:55.520
It is not a little homunculus sitting in the control room of your brain making decisions.

31:55.680 --> 31:56.400
Okay, so what am I?

31:56.760 --> 32:02.280
The self is simply the highest, slowest moving layer of this generative prediction hierarchy.

32:02.540 --> 32:04.800
The slowest moving layer. What does that mean?

32:04.800 --> 32:06.920
Think about the different speeds of your life.

32:07.220 --> 32:11.040
Your lower cognitive layers are predicting incredibly fast, volatile things.

32:11.220 --> 32:15.020
The specific angle of a line you're looking at, the immediate pitch of a dog barking,

32:15.360 --> 32:17.260
the texture of the keyboard you are touching.

32:17.680 --> 32:19.000
Right, constant sensory noise.

32:19.160 --> 32:22.020
Those models update milliseconds at a time.

32:22.500 --> 32:27.400
But your highest layer predicts the most stable, long-term invariance of your existence.

32:27.800 --> 32:31.600
It produces your name, your relationships, your core values.

32:31.600 --> 32:39.520
It compresses your decades of lived experience into a stable generative schema to maintain continuity over time.

32:40.060 --> 32:43.900
Identity is just the equilibrium point that best compresses your experience.

32:44.120 --> 32:45.720
It is a functional abstraction.

32:45.720 --> 32:50.080
I've got to push back hard here because this is going to sound deeply unsettling to anyone listening.

32:50.220 --> 32:50.280
Yeah.

32:50.600 --> 32:54.120
Are we just biological equations trying to minimize surprise?

32:54.880 --> 32:59.900
Does Fliction seriously think my personality, my childhood memories, my loves, and my fears

32:59.900 --> 33:03.880
are just a stability sequence protecting my meek computer from prediction errors?

33:04.020 --> 33:05.940
It sounds like it strips away all free will.

33:06.280 --> 33:10.000
If we look strictly through the lens of Fliction's taxonomy, essentially yes.

33:10.080 --> 33:10.560
Oh, brutal.

33:10.560 --> 33:12.960
The self is described as a predictive interface.

33:13.280 --> 33:17.280
Its job is to negotiate alignment between the internal biological states of your body

33:17.280 --> 33:20.100
and the external complex demands of society.

33:20.520 --> 33:25.060
You maintain your identity by minimizing surprise over long temporal horizons.

33:25.320 --> 33:29.320
But wait, if minimizing surprise is the ultimate goal,

33:29.580 --> 33:34.120
if the brain just wants to perfectly predict everything and have zero errors,

33:34.960 --> 33:37.340
why do humans do unpredictable things?

33:37.480 --> 33:38.100
What do you mean?

33:38.100 --> 33:43.760
Why do we explore new cities or create avant-garde art or jump out of perfectly good airplanes?

33:44.460 --> 33:50.020
Wouldn't the ultimate way to minimize surprise just be to sit in a dark, quiet room forever

33:50.020 --> 33:51.040
where nothing changes?

33:51.440 --> 33:55.240
You've just hit on the exact paradox that predictive coding has to solve,

33:55.380 --> 33:58.500
and it solves it through what the text calls active inference.

33:59.280 --> 34:02.860
The brain doesn't just passively update its internal model to match the world.

34:02.960 --> 34:03.320
It doesn't.

34:03.420 --> 34:05.380
Sometimes it takes the opposite route.

34:05.380 --> 34:09.340
It modifies the environment to make its own internal predictions come true.

34:09.840 --> 34:12.160
Flexion calls this interface control.

34:12.320 --> 34:13.120
Modify the environment.

34:13.200 --> 34:14.880
Okay, give me an example of interface control.

34:15.120 --> 34:18.380
Let's say you have a strong, slow-moving prediction about yourself.

34:18.620 --> 34:22.060
I am a person who drinks a hot cup of coffee every morning at 7 a.m.

34:22.960 --> 34:26.720
You wake up, you walk into the kitchen, and you see the coffee pot is empty.

34:26.820 --> 34:27.240
A tragedy.

34:27.280 --> 34:29.160
You have a massive prediction error.

34:29.600 --> 34:31.540
The world does not match your model.

34:31.540 --> 34:33.960
Now, you could just update your belief manifold.

34:34.160 --> 34:38.360
You could say, oh, I guess I am a person who doesn't drink coffee anymore, and the error

34:38.360 --> 34:39.060
would go away.

34:39.360 --> 34:40.580
But I would never do that.

34:40.700 --> 34:41.900
I would be miserable.

34:42.100 --> 34:42.420
Exactly.

34:42.860 --> 34:45.860
Instead of changing your belief, you use interface control.

34:46.080 --> 34:49.820
You physically move your body, you grind the beans, you pour the water, and you brew the

34:49.820 --> 34:50.120
coffee.

34:50.360 --> 34:55.260
You force the physical world to change so that it perfectly matches your prediction.

34:55.260 --> 34:57.420
Oh, that is brilliant.

34:58.020 --> 35:03.800
Action is just the organism modifying the boundary conditions of the world so that its internal

35:03.800 --> 35:06.160
generative rules remain valid.

35:06.760 --> 35:09.140
I am enforcing my abstractions upon the environment.

35:09.300 --> 35:09.560
Yes.

35:09.880 --> 35:12.360
You are acting to maintain your normal form.

35:12.880 --> 35:15.220
And this explains so much about human psychology.

35:15.500 --> 35:20.020
Because when this predictive system fails, we see what clinical psychiatry calls identity

35:20.020 --> 35:21.340
disorders or trauma.

35:21.540 --> 35:23.140
Trauma is a prediction failure.

35:23.140 --> 35:25.420
Felician defines these conditions geometrically.

35:25.880 --> 35:30.400
A severe trauma or a maladaptive habit is a literal failure in the geometry of belief.

35:30.680 --> 35:32.720
How does trauma look as a geometry problem?

35:33.060 --> 35:35.480
It might present as manifold fragmentation.

35:36.080 --> 35:37.800
Imagine that landscape we talked about earlier.

35:38.060 --> 35:40.380
But instead of a smooth surface, it is shattered.

35:40.820 --> 35:46.900
The brain develops multiple completely incompatible identity attractors to deal with contradictory

35:46.900 --> 35:48.320
painful environments.

35:48.320 --> 35:54.320
Or it might present as a state of hyperactive prediction errors, where the brain is constantly

35:54.320 --> 35:59.640
predicting danger that isn't there, meaning no stable fixed point, no normal form can

35:59.640 --> 36:00.120
be found.

36:00.240 --> 36:00.840
It can't settle.

36:01.060 --> 36:05.160
The person is trapped in an infinite loop of beta reduction that never finishes.

36:05.460 --> 36:08.860
So trauma literally shatters the geometric surface of the self.

36:08.980 --> 36:09.100
Yeah.

36:09.180 --> 36:10.740
The predictive interface breaks down.

36:11.160 --> 36:12.160
The batter never bakes.

36:12.160 --> 36:12.720
Exactly.

36:13.280 --> 36:14.860
The self is not a given right.

36:15.640 --> 36:18.820
It is enacted, computed, and continuously maintained.

36:19.200 --> 36:23.040
And when it cannot resolve its internal computations, the abstraction fails.

36:23.160 --> 36:24.780
That is breathtakingly clinical.

36:25.280 --> 36:28.820
But somehow it feels deeply resonant and epithetic at the same time.

36:29.240 --> 36:31.820
But it also leads us directly into some very dark territory.

36:31.960 --> 36:32.260
It does.

36:32.260 --> 36:35.060
The human brain uses abstraction to survive.

36:35.860 --> 36:41.040
It reduces the overwhelming complexity of the world into manageable models so we don't

36:41.040 --> 36:42.620
go insane from sensory overload.

36:43.360 --> 36:48.620
But what happens when human beings take these exact same mathematical reduction tactics and

36:48.620 --> 36:49.780
apply them to other humans?

36:50.040 --> 36:52.000
What happens when we abstract people?

36:52.420 --> 36:55.100
This is perhaps the most crucial part of Fliction's work.

36:55.580 --> 37:01.080
The text transitions from the mechanics of cognition into a strict taxonomy of ethics.

37:01.080 --> 37:02.400
Ethics of abstraction.

37:02.760 --> 37:03.340
That's a new one.

37:03.500 --> 37:07.480
It outlines the profound difference between ethical abstraction and what Fliction calls

37:07.480 --> 37:09.840
extractive abstraction, or extraction.

37:10.400 --> 37:11.700
Let's break down that taxonomy.

37:12.440 --> 37:14.940
We've established that abstraction is necessary to function.

37:15.320 --> 37:16.880
So what makes an abstraction ethical?

37:17.260 --> 37:22.040
An ethical abstraction simplifies a complex structure to make it usable, but it actively

37:22.040 --> 37:23.700
remembers what it is forgetting.

37:23.900 --> 37:25.300
Remembers what it is forgetting.

37:25.580 --> 37:27.900
Think of a well-designed software interface.

37:27.900 --> 37:33.660
It hides the messy implementation details from you so you can do your work, but the system

37:33.660 --> 37:38.860
architecture respects that those details exist, that they are valid, and that they are necessary

37:38.860 --> 37:40.040
for the whole thing to run.

37:40.800 --> 37:44.760
An ethical abstraction knows it is just a map, not the territory.

37:44.920 --> 37:45.460
An extraction?

37:45.580 --> 37:47.240
What makes abstraction turn violent?

37:47.320 --> 37:51.920
Extraction is when the abstraction is taken as the entirety of a thing's identity.

37:52.400 --> 37:56.140
It is when you completely ignore all of the entity's interdependencies.

37:56.140 --> 37:57.260
Like it doesn't exist.

37:57.400 --> 38:02.420
It is the arrogant belief that the reduced, simplified invariant is the only thing that

38:02.420 --> 38:04.660
is real, or the only thing that has value.

38:05.000 --> 38:08.880
I think the text gives a really stark example regarding nature.

38:09.820 --> 38:12.520
Reducing a forest purely to a carbon metric.

38:12.700 --> 38:12.980
Yes.

38:13.260 --> 38:14.940
That is a perfect example of extraction.

38:15.500 --> 38:20.160
When a government or a corporation looks at an ancient, complex forest and abstracts it

38:20.160 --> 38:24.020
purely as a timber reserve or a carbon sink, detail is removed.

38:24.120 --> 38:25.460
Which is mathematically abstraction.

38:25.460 --> 38:27.000
It's a normal form, yes.

38:27.620 --> 38:32.700
But extraction occurs when that single numerical representation is used to justify policy,

38:33.180 --> 38:38.640
completely ignoring the complex ecological web, the soil microbiomes, the animal habitats,

38:38.980 --> 38:42.140
the indigenous history, that the forest actually is.

38:42.220 --> 38:43.860
The metric replaces the reality.

38:44.260 --> 38:44.720
Exactly.

38:45.360 --> 38:48.320
Flexion refers to this as the ontology of ignorance.

38:48.320 --> 38:55.520
And I should note, we are just exploring Flexion's philosophical taxonomy here without endorsing a specific political side.

38:55.640 --> 38:57.820
We are just looking at the mechanics of the claim.

38:57.980 --> 38:58.180
Right.

38:58.300 --> 38:58.740
Absolutely.

38:58.960 --> 39:00.620
We're impartially exploring the text.

39:01.140 --> 39:10.340
But the phrase ontology of ignorance is so striking, meaning you have to actively work to stay stupid about the complexity in order to extract value from it.

39:10.480 --> 39:10.960
Precisely.

39:10.960 --> 39:15.320
You must aggressively forget the interdependencies of a system to exploit it.

39:15.320 --> 39:19.940
The text uses two really evocative, almost poetic phrases here that I want to explore.

39:20.080 --> 39:21.740
The closed eye and the broken back.

39:22.360 --> 39:24.440
What do those refer to in this ethical framework?

39:24.860 --> 39:28.460
They are metaphors for the systemic violence of extractive abstraction.

39:28.460 --> 39:33.000
The closed eye refers to a kind of phenomenological bracketing.

39:33.000 --> 39:38.420
It is when someone simplifies the world not to understand it better, but to actively avoid it.

39:38.760 --> 39:39.620
Avoid the consequences.

39:39.860 --> 39:40.100
Yes.

39:40.480 --> 39:47.000
Looking away from the moral consequences of an action by reducing the world to a simplified model of one's own will or convenience.

39:47.440 --> 39:49.480
I immediately think of modern warfare.

39:50.040 --> 39:58.120
Like a drone pilot sitting in a trailer thousands of miles away, treating a human target as just a cluster of gray pixels on a screen.

39:58.120 --> 39:59.640
It's a very real example.

39:59.880 --> 40:03.460
The interface has abstracted away the heat, the sound, the humanity of the target.

40:03.600 --> 40:04.380
It's a closed eye.

40:04.740 --> 40:07.640
That is a very stark, accurate application of the concept.

40:08.200 --> 40:13.640
The interface allows the pilot to execute a task without engaging with the unabstracted reality of the action.

40:14.020 --> 40:17.780
Now, the broken back refers to architectural or systemic abstraction.

40:18.000 --> 40:18.900
Like building a city.

40:19.120 --> 40:21.720
Fliction uses the metaphor of building a monument.

40:21.720 --> 40:33.360
When an architect or a ruler cares only for the grand finished pyramid, the muriological whole, they abstract the human workers into generic, interchangeable, expendable units of labor.

40:33.580 --> 40:35.280
The workers just become numbers.

40:35.700 --> 40:43.600
The fragility, the exhaustion, and the suffering of the individual human part is completely erased in favor of the stabilized whole.

40:43.600 --> 40:47.780
And fliction specifically targets modern bureaucracy with this concept, right?

40:47.880 --> 40:51.320
There's a whole section warning of the bureaucratic fallacy.

40:51.480 --> 40:55.920
Again, remaining impartial, what is the mechanical argument against bureaucracy here?

40:55.920 --> 40:56.260
Yes.

40:56.780 --> 41:14.820
The bureaucratic fallacy is the dangerous assumption that because a complex human being has been successfully rendered as a simplified metric on a spreadsheet, a credit score, a student ID, a patient billing, code optimizing that specific metric is equivalent to improving the person's actual life.

41:14.900 --> 41:17.360
Which we know from experience is almost never true.

41:17.360 --> 41:28.980
Exactly. Bureaucracy, in fiction's view, is a form of violent abstraction because it systematically discards the rich history, the kinship networks, and the ecological embeddedness of a person.

41:29.420 --> 41:33.460
It retains only what is administratively useful to the state or the corporation.

41:33.800 --> 41:37.680
And when policies are enacted on the abstraction rather than the messy reality.

41:38.000 --> 41:42.520
The results can be catastrophic. The abstraction breaks the back of the reality it sits on.

41:42.520 --> 41:54.020
So if a doctor is forced by an insurance company to treat you not as a person in pain, but as a specific billing code that only allows for a 15-minute consultation, that is extractive abstraction.

41:54.220 --> 41:54.460
Right.

41:55.020 --> 42:00.940
But wait. If computational extraction is the root of this bureaucratic problem, what is the solution?

42:02.040 --> 42:06.380
The text introduces a whole school of philosophy here. Phenomenology.

42:07.140 --> 42:09.460
Thinkers like Husserl, Heidegger, and Merleau-Ponty.

42:09.460 --> 42:15.120
Are these philosophers the heroes of Fliction's story? Are they fighting back against the lambda calculus?

42:15.460 --> 42:23.160
It's a fascinating dynamic. Fliction presents phenomenology as the absolute polar antithesis of computational abstraction.

42:23.840 --> 42:37.840
Now, it is a very common misconception in academic circles that Edmund Husserl's famous phenomenological epoche, which is the suspension or bracketing of our natural assumptions about the world, is similar to a lambda calculus reduction.

42:37.840 --> 42:39.780
Because they both use the word reduction.

42:39.900 --> 42:45.240
Right. People assume they are the same mechanical process, but Fliction argues vehemently against this.

42:45.300 --> 42:48.300
Why? If they're both reducing things, aren't they doing the same work?

42:48.560 --> 42:54.500
Because they move in opposite directions. Computational abstraction, as we've discussed, thins the world.

42:54.500 --> 43:03.440
It actively eliminates detail. It burns away the messy context to find a functional, stable invariant, a normal form.

43:03.640 --> 43:03.820
Right.

43:03.980 --> 43:07.000
But phenomenological reduction thickens the world.

43:07.160 --> 43:10.060
Thickens it. How do you thicken the world by reducing your assumptions?

43:10.060 --> 43:17.600
When Husserl suspends his presuppositions, he isn't doing it to simplify his experience into a clean data point.

43:18.120 --> 43:25.680
He is doing it to strip away his biases so he can dive back into the overwhelming, dense, chaotic multiplicity of lived experience.

43:25.900 --> 43:29.800
He wants to feel the raw texture of consciousness before it gets categorized.

43:30.000 --> 43:38.420
Ah, I see. So if abstraction is putting things in neat little boxes, phenomenology is violently tearing the cardboard boxes apart and swimming in the messy pile of stuff on the floor.

43:38.480 --> 43:39.600
That's one way to put it, yeah.

43:39.600 --> 43:47.560
Is fliction suggesting that philosophers are purposely trying to break our mental software by refusing to let things reduce into normal forms?

43:47.560 --> 43:57.720
In a profound way, yes. Phenomenology points out that human experience is a trajectory space that fiercely resists being flattened into a simple, normal form.

43:58.340 --> 44:00.140
Think of Maurice Merleau-Ponty.

44:00.580 --> 44:01.220
What did he argue?

44:01.600 --> 44:05.200
He argued that the physical human body is a site of irreducible thickness.

44:05.200 --> 44:15.580
You cannot abstract the embodied, sweaty, pulsing human experience into a clean, input-output interface without severe, violent distortion.

44:16.420 --> 44:20.600
Phenomenology is a rebellion against the flattening impulse of structural analysis.

44:20.860 --> 44:22.980
It is the batter refusing to become the Kate.

44:23.160 --> 44:27.860
Exactly. It is the demand to remain in the complex, unresolved state of becoming.

44:27.860 --> 44:31.160
But fliction isn't saying we should abandon abstraction entirely, right?

44:31.220 --> 44:36.680
Because we established in the previous sections that we literally need it to think, to predict, to survive.

44:36.880 --> 44:39.920
If we don't abstract, we drown in sensory overload.

44:40.160 --> 44:43.120
Correct. We cannot survive without epistemic compression.

44:43.680 --> 44:45.340
The brain must beta-reduce to function.

44:46.040 --> 44:50.280
So fliction's ultimate conclusion in this section is a call for an ethical theory of abstraction.

44:50.280 --> 44:57.200
An ethical abstraction must reduce internal complexity only to the degree strictly required for coherence.

44:58.080 --> 45:02.540
And crucially, it must never, ever treat the abstraction as the whole truth.

45:02.840 --> 45:06.780
It must always allow the underlying reality to veto the abstraction.

45:07.120 --> 45:09.580
Allow the underlying reality to veto the abstraction.

45:09.880 --> 45:10.720
I love that.

45:11.060 --> 45:14.720
When the map doesn't match the territory, you don't burn the territory.

45:15.000 --> 45:17.300
You trust the territory and redraw the map.

45:17.360 --> 45:17.880
Exactly.

45:17.880 --> 45:20.420
Okay. We have traveled an immense distance.

45:21.040 --> 45:30.800
We started in the pure math of code, moved into the physical wiring of hardware, scaled up to the geometry of the human mind, and navigated the moral ethics of how we treat each other.

45:30.840 --> 45:32.000
It's a massive scope.

45:32.160 --> 45:34.720
It feels like we've covered the entire spectrum of existence.

45:35.840 --> 45:41.120
But the final part of Fliction's text takes us into the ultimate, staggering unification.

45:41.660 --> 45:44.300
We're going all the way down to the hard physics.

45:44.440 --> 45:45.400
It's sphere-pub calculus.

45:45.400 --> 45:51.040
Right. How does this universal rule of reduction literally manifest in the fabric of space and time?

45:51.320 --> 45:52.840
Walk me through the sphere-pub calculus.

45:53.220 --> 45:56.180
Let's dive in, because this is where the theory becomes truly cosmic.

45:57.000 --> 46:01.540
Sphere-pub is a novel geometric process language introduced by Fliction.

46:02.120 --> 46:07.740
Up until now, we've thought of computation as lines of code on a screen or equations on a blackboard.

46:08.480 --> 46:13.320
Sphere-pub asks us to visualize computation as physical, spatial regions.

46:13.320 --> 46:18.500
Okay. I'm going to need a very strong visual for this. Are we talking about geometric shapes?

46:18.820 --> 46:23.420
Let's visualize them as floating, glowing bubbles or spheres of space.

46:23.920 --> 46:26.940
These spheres aren't empty. They carry payloads of information.

46:27.520 --> 46:31.340
And computation in the sphere-pub language doesn't happen by writing out numbers.

46:31.580 --> 46:33.960
It happens entirely through spatial geometric manipulation.

46:34.640 --> 46:35.620
Like interacting bubbles.

46:35.760 --> 46:37.420
Right. There are four primitive operations.

46:37.560 --> 46:38.440
What can the bubbles do?

46:38.440 --> 46:43.040
First is merge, where two distinct spheres physically overlap, combine, and interact.

46:43.420 --> 46:50.560
Second is collapse, where a complex configuration of multiple bubbles is suddenly reduced or abstracted into a single, simpler sphere.

46:50.700 --> 46:52.040
Okay. Merge and collapse.

46:52.320 --> 46:57.140
Third is scale, where a sphere changes size to encompass more or less space.

46:57.520 --> 47:02.860
And fourth is pipe, which creates a tunnel that feeds the contents of one region into a process.

47:02.860 --> 47:09.340
So instead of adding two numbers on a chalkboard with chalk, you are physically smashing two glowing bubbles together.

47:09.600 --> 47:12.560
And they pop and merge into a new single shape.

47:12.840 --> 47:14.980
It's like doing math with soap suds.

47:15.380 --> 47:17.900
That is a beautifully intuitive way to grasp it.

47:18.020 --> 47:24.060
And mathematically, fliction proves in the text that this geometric bubble math is fully Turing-complete.

47:24.260 --> 47:26.840
Meaning it can compute anything a supercomputer can compute.

47:26.840 --> 47:30.260
Exactly. It can simulate the lambda calculus perfectly.

47:30.600 --> 47:35.520
The physical merging and collapsing of these spatial regions is structurally identical to beta reduction.

47:36.040 --> 47:42.940
When a cluster of complex bubbles collapses into a single, stable sphere, that is a geometric normal form.

47:43.500 --> 47:45.240
Okay. But why invent bubble math?

47:45.380 --> 47:46.740
I mean, the lambda calculus works fine.

47:47.020 --> 47:50.000
Why do we need to translate code into floating spatial spheres?

47:50.200 --> 47:51.500
What does this do for the grand theory?

47:51.500 --> 47:58.320
It allows us to visualize computation as a physical flow through geometry, which is necessary to connect it to physics.

47:58.820 --> 48:01.840
Fliction introduces a concept here called semantic DAGs.

48:02.080 --> 48:05.320
A DAG. That's an acronym. Directed Acyclic Graph.

48:05.440 --> 48:11.220
Right. Think of a DAG as a flowchart or a pipeline where information only flows in one forward direction.

48:11.760 --> 48:13.360
It never loops back on itself.

48:14.080 --> 48:18.240
In sphere pop, a semantic DAG is a layered pipeline of meaning.

48:18.240 --> 48:23.860
Each layer in this geometric pipeline acts as a semi-permeable Markov boundary.

48:24.600 --> 48:28.120
A semi-permeable Markov boundary. That sounds like a shield on a spaceship.

48:28.480 --> 48:30.000
Let's break that down into plain English.

48:30.420 --> 48:32.680
A Markov boundary is a concept from statistics.

48:33.200 --> 48:36.380
It's a membrane that separates internal states from external states.

48:37.060 --> 48:41.560
The key property is that all influence from the outside world must pass through the boundary.

48:41.880 --> 48:42.700
So it's a filter.

48:42.700 --> 48:48.680
It acts exactly like a filter. It strips away the noise and only lets the structurally relevant information through.

48:49.120 --> 48:53.160
Can you ground this in human experience? What does a Markov boundary feel like?

48:53.380 --> 48:56.680
You are experiencing it right now. Think about the sound of my voice.

48:57.520 --> 49:01.900
Raw sound waves, vibrations in the air, are hitting your eardrum.

49:01.900 --> 49:05.540
That is massive, chaotic, high-dimensional data.

49:06.380 --> 49:11.100
But as that data flows through the neural DAG in your brain, it hits Markov boundaries.

49:11.260 --> 49:15.140
The first boundary filters out the background hiss of the room and extract syllables.

49:15.720 --> 49:18.980
The next boundary scales those syllables and merges them into words.

49:19.240 --> 49:22.740
The next boundary collapses those words into abstract concepts.

49:23.180 --> 49:25.420
So raw physics goes one end of the pipeline.

49:25.540 --> 49:27.500
It gets smashed and collapsed by these boundaries.

49:27.980 --> 49:29.520
And abstract meaning comes out the other end.

49:29.520 --> 49:35.220
Yes. As the data flows through the boundaries, it gets staled, twisted, and folded, just like the sphere-pop bubbles.

49:35.440 --> 49:39.960
The raw data is physically compressed into higher and higher levels of abstract meaning.

49:40.140 --> 49:41.200
And how does physics fit in?

49:41.500 --> 49:45.800
Now here is where Flixson grounds this geometric flow in actual quantum mechanics.

49:45.980 --> 49:49.900
